1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,564
In Episode 1, Zoya made fifty
laddoos and sold only eight.

2
00:00:04,864 --> 00:00:09,166
Now, Laddoo Lab sells in shops,
a supermarket chain and online.

3
00:00:09,716 --> 00:00:11,813
But is the marketing really working?

4
00:00:11,813 --> 00:00:13,292
Zoya can't just feel it.

5
00:00:13,292 --> 00:00:14,728
She has to measure it.

6
00:00:15,378 --> 00:00:18,918
So at the end of the year, the
team gathers at Nani's table.

7
00:00:19,268 --> 00:00:20,966
Arjun brings the sales.

8
00:00:20,966 --> 00:00:23,896
Dev brings the ads and the survey replies.

9
00:00:23,896 --> 00:00:26,287
And Nani asks: are people happy?

10
00:00:26,637 --> 00:00:29,846
Together, they build Laddoo
Lab's first yearly scorecard.

11
00:00:30,496 --> 00:00:33,019
So what are marketing performance metrics?

12
00:00:33,319 --> 00:00:36,435
They are numbers that show
whether your marketing is working.

13
00:00:36,785 --> 00:00:40,490
A good metric has three parts:
a number, a goal, and a time.

14
00:00:40,490 --> 00:00:43,775
Like: keep eighty percent of
regular families, this year.

15
00:00:44,425 --> 00:00:48,879
Let's build the scorecard, one metric
at a time, with example figures.

16
00:00:49,229 --> 00:00:50,129
One.

17
00:00:49,752 --> 00:00:50,798
Sales growth.

18
00:00:50,798 --> 00:00:53,156
Last year, thirty thousand packs.

19
00:00:53,156 --> 00:00:55,463
This year, thirty nine thousand.

20
00:00:55,813 --> 00:01:00,515
Nine thousand more, divided by thirty
thousand, is thirty percent growth.

21
00:01:00,515 --> 00:01:02,292
The goal was twenty five.

22
00:01:02,292 --> 00:01:03,192
Green.

23
00:01:03,245 --> 00:01:04,145
Two.

24
00:01:03,768 --> 00:01:05,121
Customer retention.

25
00:01:05,121 --> 00:01:09,592
Of two hundred regular families,
one hundred and seventy still buy.

26
00:01:09,942 --> 00:01:11,545
That's eighty five percent.

27
00:01:11,545 --> 00:01:12,879
The goal was eighty.

28
00:01:12,879 --> 00:01:13,779
Green.

29
00:01:13,775 --> 00:01:14,675
Three.

30
00:01:14,431 --> 00:01:16,258
Cost to win a customer.

31
00:01:16,258 --> 00:01:21,557
Fifty thousand rupees on trial packs and
ads brought one hundred new families.

32
00:01:21,907 --> 00:01:23,816
That's five hundred rupees each.

33
00:01:23,816 --> 00:01:25,498
The goal was four hundred.

34
00:01:25,498 --> 00:01:26,398
Red.

35
00:01:26,311 --> 00:01:27,211
Four.

36
00:01:26,907 --> 00:01:28,430
Return on marketing.

37
00:01:28,430 --> 00:01:33,400
The festive campaign cost forty thousand
rupees, and brought one lakh rupees

38
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:34,710
of extra profit.

39
00:01:35,060 --> 00:01:39,920
One lakh, minus forty thousand,
divided by forty thousand, is a hundred

40
00:01:39,920 --> 00:01:41,319
and fifty percent.

41
00:01:41,319 --> 00:01:42,219
Green.

42
00:01:42,610 --> 00:01:44,571
What you measure, you can improve.

43
00:01:45,271 --> 00:01:48,862
Nani's question gets its own
number: the Net Promoter Score.

44
00:01:49,212 --> 00:01:51,833
Fred Reichheld introduced it in 2003.

45
00:01:51,833 --> 00:01:56,767
Ask customers: how likely are you
to recommend us, from zero to ten?

46
00:01:57,117 --> 00:01:59,043
Nine or ten are promoters.

47
00:01:59,043 --> 00:02:01,074
Seven or eight are passives.

48
00:02:01,074 --> 00:02:03,104
Six or below are detractors.

49
00:02:03,454 --> 00:02:05,988
Laddoo Lab asks a hundred customers.

50
00:02:05,988 --> 00:02:10,676
Sixty promoters, minus ten detractors,
gives a score of plus fifty.

51
00:02:11,326 --> 00:02:13,897
Where do we see these
metrics at work today?

52
00:02:14,247 --> 00:02:15,231
Sales growth.

53
00:02:15,231 --> 00:02:18,751
A phone maker compares its
growth with the whole market.

54
00:02:19,101 --> 00:02:20,001
Retention.

55
00:02:19,893 --> 00:02:23,959
A bank watches how many customers
close their accounts each year.

56
00:02:24,309 --> 00:02:25,682
Return on marketing.

57
00:02:25,682 --> 00:02:29,342
A food delivery app checks the
profit each ad brings back.

58
00:02:29,692 --> 00:02:31,002
Net Promoter Score.

59
00:02:31,002 --> 00:02:34,315
An airline asks travellers
if they would recommend it.

60
00:02:34,965 --> 00:02:36,856
But numbers can fool you too.

61
00:02:37,206 --> 00:02:38,230
The good side.

62
00:02:38,230 --> 00:02:41,394
Metrics show what works,
and stop you wasting money.

63
00:02:41,744 --> 00:02:42,770
The hard side.

64
00:02:42,770 --> 00:02:47,246
Some numbers look good but mean little,
and people start chasing the number.

65
00:02:47,596 --> 00:02:49,779
Dev's post likes doubled this year.

66
00:02:49,779 --> 00:02:52,009
But orders from posts barely moved.

67
00:02:52,359 --> 00:02:57,174
So Zoya drops likes from the scorecard,
and keeps orders and repeat buyers.

68
00:02:57,524 --> 00:03:00,342
In 1997, Marilyn Strathern put it well.

69
00:03:00,342 --> 00:03:04,828
When a measure becomes a target,
it ceases to be a good measure.

70
00:03:05,178 --> 00:03:06,078
The lesson?

71
00:03:05,999 --> 00:03:08,935
Measure what matters, not
what is easy to count.

72
00:03:09,585 --> 00:03:10,509
Quick check.

73
00:03:10,509 --> 00:03:14,758
Of two hundred regular families,
one hundred and seventy keep buying.

74
00:03:14,758 --> 00:03:16,568
What is the retention rate?

75
00:03:18,468 --> 00:03:19,891
Eighty five percent!

76
00:03:19,891 --> 00:03:23,105
One hundred and seventy,
divided by two hundred.

77
00:03:23,705 --> 00:03:24,714
So, remember.

78
00:03:25,014 --> 00:03:28,596
Metrics are numbers that show
whether your marketing is working.

79
00:03:28,947 --> 00:03:33,184
Track growth, retention, cost to
win, return on marketing, and the Net

80
00:03:33,184 --> 00:03:34,201
Promoter Score.

81
00:03:34,550 --> 00:03:37,213
Measure what matters, and
act on what you learn.

82
00:03:37,813 --> 00:03:39,387
That's the full series.

83
00:03:39,387 --> 00:03:43,882
From fifty laddoos in Nani's kitchen
to a brand in shops and online, you

84
00:03:43,882 --> 00:03:46,674
now know the heart of
marketing management.
